      "method": "v1.1.0 case-law rebuild: authorities selected per decision point, each opinion retrieved from CourtListener and read; holding and quote taken from the opinion text; independently re-verified before release. Previous keyword-matched Justia listings were removed.",
      "verified_date": "2026-09-20",
      "key_cases": [
        {
          "case_name": "Suter v. Artist M.",
          "citation": "503 U.S. 347 (1992)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of the United States",
          "year": 1992,
          "date_decided": "1992-03-25",
          "holding": "The Court held that the 'reasonable efforts' requirement of 42 U.S.C. section 671(a)(15) of the Adoption Assistance and Child Welfare Act creates no privately enforceable right, so it may not be enforced by children or families through an implied right of action or under 42 U.S.C. section 1983.",
          "relevance": "Because the reasonable-efforts requirement is not privately enforceable, federal review processes such as the CFSR are the principal compliance mechanism.",
          "quote": "We hold that the Act does not create an enforceable right on behalf of respondents.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112712/suter-v-artist-m/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 112712
        },
        {
          "case_name": "DeShaney v. Winnebago County Department of Social Services",
          "citation": "489 U.S. 189 (1989)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of the United States",
          "year": 1989,
          "date_decided": "1989-02-22",
          "holding": "The Court held that the Due Process Clause imposes no affirmative obligation on a State to protect a child from private violence by a parent, so a county's failure to remove a child it knew was being abused did not violate the Fourteenth Amendment, while reserving that a custodial relationship such as state-operated foster care could give rise to an affirmative duty.",
          "relevance": "Because constitutional liability is narrow, systemic performance monitoring rather than litigation is the principal federal check on state child-welfare failures.",
          "quote": "a State's failure to protect an individual against private violence simply does not constitute a violation of the Due Process Clause",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112202/deshaney-v-winnebago-county-department-of-social-services/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 112202
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Norfleet ex rel. Norfleet v. Arkansas Department of Human Services",
          "citation": "989 F.2d 289 (8th Cir. 1993)",
          "court": "United States Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit",
          "year": 1993,
          "date_decided": "1993-03-29",
          "holding": "The Eighth Circuit affirmed the denial of qualified immunity, holding that placing a child in foster care creates a custodial relationship and that it was clearly established that the state then owes the child adequate medical care, protection and supervision under the Due Process Clause.",
          "relevance": "Systemic failures in medical oversight of foster children are exactly the safety and well-being outcomes federal reviews are designed to measure.",
          "quote": "In foster care, a child loses his freedom and ability to make decisions about his own welfare, and must rely on the state to take care of his needs.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9015856/norfleet-ex-rel-norfleet-v-arkansas-department-of-human-services/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 9015856
        }
      ],
